Subject: llvmpipe: the thread that queues a small scene rasterizes it

Handing a scene to the rasteriser threads costs the same whatever is in it: a semaphore to wake each thread, two
barriers they all meet at, a semaphore back from each. The window server draws a frame as 60 to 77 render passes, about
50 of them a few thousand pixels (an icon, a line of menu bar text, a level of a blur), and for those the hand-off is
all of the time. Measured in the hosted macOS with METAL2VULKAN_ENCODER_TIMES, an empty pass: 0.05 ms with
LP_NUM_THREADS=0, 0.36 ms with 4, 0.6 ms with 8 (thread wakeups there cost three times what they do on the Linux host).

A scene whose commands may touch at most LP_INLINE_SCENE_PIXELS pixels (each command of a bin counts the bin's tile,
cut to the framebuffer; 32768 by default, 0 turns this off) is rasterized by the thread that queues it, as the
LP_NUM_THREADS=0 path does, when the threads have finished every scene handed to them. The caller holds the screen's
rast_mutex, so no scene is queued meanwhile, and thread 0 is waiting for work, so its task is free.

